// Practice quiz: JavaScript for Java developers
// First draft: unreviewed. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: html-css-js/javascript-fundamentals/javascript-for-java-developers

::o1 mixing number types::A script runs\: const total \= 5n + 2; What happens? {
=It throws a TypeError #Correct. BigInt and Number cannot be mixed in arithmetic without an explicit conversion.
~total becomes 7n #Not quite. JavaScript does not widen Number to BigInt automatically.
~total becomes 7 #Not quite. BigInt is not narrowed to Number automatically either.
~It fails to compile, as in Java #Not quite. There is no compile-time type check; the failure happens when the line runs.
}

::o1 where the type lives::Which statement best contrasts JavaScript with Java? {
=JavaScript values carry their types, and a variable can hold any type over time #Correct. That is dynamic typing\: nothing checks the variable against a declared type.
~JavaScript variables are declared with a type that is checked at run time #Not quite. JavaScript variables have no declared type at all.
~JavaScript has separate int and double types, inferred automatically #Not quite. Ordinary numbers all share one Number type.
~JavaScript converts every value to a string when it is stored #Not quite. Values keep their own types when stored.
}

::o2 detecting an array::You need to test whether a value is an array. Which test works? {
=Array.isArray(value) #Correct. It returns true only for arrays.
~typeof value \=\=\= "array" #Not quite. typeof never returns the string array.
~typeof value \=\=\= "object" #Not quite. That is also true for plain objects and for null.
~value \=\= null #Not quite. That only tests for null or undefined.
}

::o2 surprising typeof::What does typeof null return? {
=the string object #Correct. This is a long-standing quirk, so test for null with strict equality instead.
~the string null #Not quite. There is no null result from typeof.
~the string undefined #Not quite. That is the result for the undefined value, not for null.
~It throws a TypeError #Not quite. typeof never throws for null.
}

::o3 reading a crash::node app.js prints two lines, then a TypeError naming app.js line 9. What do you conclude about line 10? {
=It never ran, because an uncaught error stops the process #Correct. Nothing after the uncaught error runs.
~It ran, because errors are only warnings in Node.js #Not quite. An uncaught error ends the run.
~It ran first, because Node.js reads files from the bottom #Not quite. Statements run from top to bottom.
~It cannot be known without recompiling #Not quite. There is no separate compile step to run here.
}
